  "close_rendering": "In the beginning God created the heavens and the earth.",
  "whats_happening": "The Bible opens with seven Hebrew words naming a beginning, an act, the one acting and two objects. They do not say how, from what, or how long. The sentence is so simple that it leaves most of a modern reader's questions unanswered, on purpose. One open question is about the first word. Does it start a full sentence ('In the beginning God created') or a 'when' phrase ('When God began to create')?",
  "key_words": [
    {
      "surface": "בְּרֵאשִׁ֖ית",
      "lemma": "רֵאשִׁית",
      "gloss": "beginning",
      "note": "A noun meaning first or chief part, with bə- ('in') joined to the front. The verse's central question is whether it stands alone or links to the words after it.",
      "strongs": "H7225"
    },
    {
      "surface": "בָּרָ֣א",
      "lemma": "בָּרָא",
      "gloss": "he created",
      "note": "In this form, the Hebrew Bible uses the verb with God as its subject. It never says what the thing was made from.",
      "strongs": "H1254a"
    },
    {
      "surface": "אֱלֹהִ֑ים",
      "lemma": "אֱלֹהִים",
      "gloss": "God",
      "note": "Plural in form, but here it takes a singular verb ('he created'). That is the usual pattern when it means the God of Israel.",
      "strongs": "H0430"
    },
    {
      "surface": "אֵ֥ת",
      "lemma": "אֵת",
      "gloss": "[direct object]",
      "note": "A small word with no English equivalent that marks the specific thing a verb acts on. It appears twice, so 'the heavens' and 'the earth' are both what God created.",
      "strongs": "H0853"
    }
  ],
  "translation_note": "Hebrew verbs show whether an action is complete, not whether it is past, so English must pick a tense. Both readings of the opening words fit the written letters, which had no vowels.",
